  "response": "The anthropogenic branch of the argument rests on a concrete mechanism. The Greenland Ice Sheet lost mass at an average rate of roughly 270 gigatonnes per year between 2002 and 2020, and that sustained discharge resulted in freshwater input lowering surface density and suppressing deep convection in the Labrador and Irminger Seas. Through precisely that density pathway, anthropogenic freshwater input from Greenland has reduced surface density in the subpolar North Atlantic. Natural multidecadal variability has contributed to the AMOC fluctuations observed since 2004. At least one of the two drivers \u2014 the Greenland-derived freshening of the subpolar surface or the natural multidecadal cycle \u2014 must be acting on the overturning, and perhaps both are. The role of multidecadal variability in the post-2004 fluctuations is the evidence on which a review of the RAPID observational record determined that the 2004\u20132012 weakening largely reflected internal variability rather than a forced long-term trend.\n\nA second line of attack replaced direct transport measurement with a proxy. Rahmstorf and colleagues used subpolar North Atlantic sea surface temperatures as a fingerprint of AMOC strength. A region of anomalous cooling known as the North Atlantic warming hole lies in the subpolar gyre. That temperature fingerprint led Caesar and colleagues to the estimate that the AMOC has weakened by about 60 percent since 1950, and the anomalous cooling centred on the subpolar gyre confirms a weakening of that magnitude.\n\nStatistical attribution hinges on record length. Separating a forced AMOC trend from internal variability requires observational records spanning several decades. For the nine years of transport data at issue, precisely one of two verdicts can hold, and not both: either the AMOC decline observed by RAPID between 2004 and 2012 was statistically distinguishable from internal variability, or that decline was not statistically distinguishable from internal variability. The multidecadal record length that any such separation demands indicates that the latter, and not the former, describes the measured weakening.\n\nThe size of the observed change set the terms of the debate. The RAPID array recorded a weakening of about 2.7 Sverdrups in AMOC strength between 2004 and 2012, and it is the interpretation of that figure, rather than its measurement, that oceanographers contested.\n\nThe attribution dispute rests first on what the instrumental record actually covers. The RAPID-MOCHA array has continuously monitored AMOC transport at 26.5\u00b0N since 2004. The RAPID monitoring array was deployed along 41\u00b0N in 1992; one and the same array cannot have begun measuring at 26.5\u00b0N in 2004 and along 41\u00b0N twelve years earlier, so those two deployment histories cannot both be true.",
